00:03:17.670 --> 00:03:20.730
So as I was saying,
everything on CPU,

00:03:20.730 --> 00:03:24.740
IO starvation,
memory starvation,

00:03:24.740 --> 00:03:28.640
it's very normal to be done
when you have small machine,

00:03:28.640 --> 00:03:30.210
small set of machines.

00:03:30.210 --> 00:03:33.500
But a data center of scale,
it doesn't make much sense.

00:03:33.500 --> 00:03:37.130
When you have thousands of
machines that are running,

00:03:37.130 --> 00:03:39.470
imagine getting paged
for any of those machines

00:03:39.470 --> 00:03:41.180
running out of memory.

00:03:41.180 --> 00:03:44.803
It doesn't even make sense at
the level of the data center.

00:03:44.803 --> 00:03:46.220
People working in
the data centers

00:03:46.220 --> 00:03:48.808
should only get paged when
a certain number of machines

00:03:48.808 --> 00:03:51.350
are running out of space or when
a certain number of machines

00:03:51.350 --> 00:03:54.470
are broken, not for every
single one of those machines.

00:03:54.470 --> 00:03:55.910
When that happens,
you're actually

00:03:55.910 --> 00:03:58.340
having data center
operators running up

00:03:58.340 --> 00:04:00.170
and down in the data
center replacing

00:04:00.170 --> 00:04:01.640
a single disk on
several machines.

00:04:01.640 --> 00:04:04.430
In fact, at Google,
we have perfected

00:04:04.430 --> 00:04:07.490
the idea of repairing a
certain number of machines

00:04:07.490 --> 00:04:08.930
when they have to be repaired.

00:04:08.930 --> 00:04:12.530
And we actually trace the
path that the operator

00:04:12.530 --> 00:04:15.510
has to do in order to go
repair those machines.

00:04:15.510 --> 00:04:23.460
So single-cause alerting is
definitely not a way to do it.

00:04:23.460 --> 00:04:26.160
In fact, if you do the math--

00:04:26.160 --> 00:04:32.650
I have here a assertion
that 5,000 machines running

00:04:32.650 --> 00:04:35.440
for a single day is
more than 10 years

00:04:35.440 --> 00:04:36.870
of a single machine running.

00:04:36.870 --> 00:04:38.480
It took 10 years.

00:04:38.480 --> 00:04:43.520
In fact, it's something
like 13.69-plus years.

00:04:43.520 --> 00:04:45.100
It's just a comparison.

00:04:45.100 --> 00:04:47.640
It's not a one-to-one
comparison.

00:04:47.640 --> 00:04:50.920
But it's to give you an idea
of how long a single machine

00:04:50.920 --> 00:04:54.160
should be running compared to
those 1,000 machines running

00:04:54.160 --> 00:04:55.085
in a data center.

00:04:55.085 --> 00:04:56.710
There are plenty of
the things that you

00:04:56.710 --> 00:05:00.280
have to take into account, like
the mean time before failure,

00:05:00.280 --> 00:05:02.140
and the bathtub curve.

00:05:02.140 --> 00:05:04.660
In the beginning, you get a
lot of failures in the machines

00:05:04.660 --> 00:05:07.790
because there's new components
and they haven't been tested.

00:05:07.790 --> 00:05:10.450
Then you have a flat
line, and then you

00:05:10.450 --> 00:05:14.920
start getting
errors and breakages

00:05:14.920 --> 00:05:17.080
at the end of the lifecycle.

00:05:17.080 --> 00:05:21.910
But essentially, the idea is
that running a very complex

00:05:21.910 --> 00:05:24.430
system with a large
set of machines

00:05:24.430 --> 00:05:27.350
increases the number of errors
that you're going to get.

00:05:27.350 --> 00:05:31.810
So it doesn't make sense at all
to page for every single one.

00:05:31.810 --> 00:05:37.180
In fact, one of the
principles, when we designed

00:05:37.180 --> 00:05:39.620
the precursor of Kubernetes--

00:05:39.620 --> 00:05:43.100
Borg-- it was that--

00:05:43.100 --> 00:05:46.220
it's not a question of
if they're going to fail,

00:05:46.220 --> 00:05:49.340
it's a question of when
they're going to fail.

00:05:49.340 --> 00:05:51.250
So essentially, what
we did at Google

00:05:51.250 --> 00:05:55.600
was incorporating
the idea of paging

00:05:55.600 --> 00:06:01.810
only when the user
experience is affected.

00:06:01.810 --> 00:06:07.270
So we move away from paging
from individual causes to paging

00:06:07.270 --> 00:06:14.110
only when there's a symptom
that reflects user happiness.

00:06:14.110 --> 00:06:15.940
Only page when the
user has been affected,

00:06:15.940 --> 00:06:18.372
when the user figures
out that there

00:06:18.372 --> 00:06:19.580
is a problem with the system.

00:06:30.435 --> 00:06:31.810
So now that we
know that we don't

00:06:31.810 --> 00:06:33.640
page for individual
machines going down,

00:06:33.640 --> 00:06:37.550
and we only page when the
users are being affected,

00:06:37.550 --> 00:06:41.410
let's talk about how we
engineer that error that we

00:06:41.410 --> 00:06:47.540
are receiving and we are seeing
in our systems, in our paging.

00:06:47.540 --> 00:06:51.050
Ben Trainer came
up with the idea

00:06:51.050 --> 00:06:54.920
that 100% is the
wrong reliability

00:06:54.920 --> 00:06:56.450
for almost everything.

00:06:56.450 --> 00:06:59.630
I would argue that it's
impossible to achieve.

00:06:59.630 --> 00:07:02.180
In order to have
100% reliability,

00:07:02.180 --> 00:07:05.270
you would have to know exactly
the state of every single

00:07:05.270 --> 00:07:08.990
of your components in your
entire system at all times

00:07:08.990 --> 00:07:12.360
during the entire lifetime
of those components.

00:07:12.360 --> 00:07:15.500
In fact, the user is not going
to know that your system is not

00:07:15.500 --> 00:07:20.030
100% reliable because once
you get into an elevator-- who

00:07:20.030 --> 00:07:22.147
has a mobile phone?

00:07:22.147 --> 00:07:24.230
How many times, during the
middle of a phone call,

00:07:24.230 --> 00:07:25.730
and then you get
into an elevator,

00:07:25.730 --> 00:07:28.980
and then you cannot
continue that phone call?

00:07:28.980 --> 00:07:30.040
Exactly.

00:07:30.040 --> 00:07:30.990
It happens.

00:07:30.990 --> 00:07:34.230
That error is introduced
not only by the system,

00:07:34.230 --> 00:07:36.300
but by your environment.

00:07:36.300 --> 00:07:42.900
So it doesn't make sense
to try to fix those errors.

00:07:42.900 --> 00:07:46.290
It's better to embrace them
because things will fail.

00:07:46.290 --> 00:07:49.500
Things will break during
the natural lifecycle

00:07:49.500 --> 00:07:51.180
of the things.

00:07:51.180 --> 00:07:53.470
You will have
hundreds of users that

00:07:53.470 --> 00:07:55.600
will try to use your
system when there

00:07:55.600 --> 00:07:59.700
is the final of the
American football, when

00:07:59.700 --> 00:08:03.280
there is an earthquake
in a different location.

00:08:03.280 --> 00:08:05.490
People will go to
your service to try

00:08:05.490 --> 00:08:07.080
to figure out what's happening.

00:08:07.080 --> 00:08:10.770
People will go to pay when
there was a dip on bitcoin

00:08:10.770 --> 00:08:14.730
or to get the bitcoins
out of your system.

00:08:14.730 --> 00:08:17.130
Also, users will try to
use your service in a way

00:08:17.130 --> 00:08:19.500
that it was not designed for.

00:08:19.500 --> 00:08:23.100
Google Maps had a
certain peak of use

00:08:23.100 --> 00:08:24.660
when people figured
out that they

00:08:24.660 --> 00:08:26.970
could get the tiles of
the maps and then start

00:08:26.970 --> 00:08:29.130
using them for other
purposes, just to put

00:08:29.130 --> 00:08:31.860
the location of your business.

00:08:31.860 --> 00:08:34.500
So we had a huge
spike on the traffic,

00:08:34.500 --> 00:08:38.740
and we had to include some
authentication to avoid that.

00:08:38.740 --> 00:08:42.000
But we had an outage
due to that problem.

00:08:42.000 --> 00:08:44.220
Also, people will
use your service

00:08:44.220 --> 00:08:47.940
in ways that were not designed,
and people will actually

00:08:47.940 --> 00:08:51.120
abuse the service by
hacking your system,

00:08:51.120 --> 00:08:54.580
by getting inside and bringing
down your database when

00:08:54.580 --> 00:08:56.100
you least suspect it.

00:08:56.100 --> 00:08:59.730
Very common to have SQL
injections that suddenly delete

00:08:59.730 --> 00:09:04.020
your entire user table, and
you cannot access your service

00:09:04.020 --> 00:09:05.170
anymore.

00:09:05.170 --> 00:09:08.970
In fact, even Google
fails every now and then.

00:09:08.970 --> 00:09:12.330
You barely notice it because
it fails very little,

00:09:12.330 --> 00:09:13.050
but it fails.

00:09:15.630 --> 00:09:19.010
So the thing that we introduced
to account for that failure

00:09:19.010 --> 00:09:21.330
is service levels.

00:09:21.330 --> 00:09:23.500
It's not avoiding error.

00:09:23.500 --> 00:09:27.120
It's introducing error
in your system in a way

00:09:27.120 --> 00:09:31.220
that you can account for it,
so that even if error exists,

00:09:31.220 --> 00:09:35.430
your users can mostly operate
and use your service in a way

00:09:35.430 --> 00:09:37.320
that it was designed for.

00:09:37.320 --> 00:09:39.630
Those concepts are probably
the most important thing

00:09:39.630 --> 00:09:42.930
that I'm introducing
today, but they're

00:09:42.930 --> 00:09:45.390
pretty much in every
single literature

00:09:45.390 --> 00:09:49.800
that we have written,
like in the two SRE books.

00:09:49.800 --> 00:09:52.740
It's a service-level indicator.

00:09:52.740 --> 00:09:53.923
It's a metric.

00:09:53.923 --> 00:09:55.840
It's the first concept
that we're introducing.

00:09:55.840 --> 00:10:00.330
It's basically a
definition of an attribute

00:10:00.330 --> 00:10:02.480
of your system, an
attribute of your system

00:10:02.480 --> 00:10:06.360
being a way that your
user is using your system.

00:10:06.360 --> 00:10:09.210
It's exactly what it says.

00:10:09.210 --> 00:10:13.320
It's an indicator of how
the service is performing

00:10:13.320 --> 00:10:15.700
at a certain point in time.

00:10:15.700 --> 00:10:21.000
An example of that would be the
proportion of HTTP GET requests

00:10:21.000 --> 00:10:22.890
that you can get
on your homepage.

00:10:22.890 --> 00:10:27.060
There are 200, 300, 400,
excluding a certain measure,

00:10:27.060 --> 00:10:28.830
but it's measured at
the load balancer.

00:10:28.830 --> 00:10:33.300
That is just the indication of
how the service is performing.

00:10:33.300 --> 00:10:36.510
A server-level objective is
a service-level indicator

00:10:36.510 --> 00:10:38.250
with a specific goal.

00:10:38.250 --> 00:10:45.360
So a goal could be that
99.9% of those requests

00:10:45.360 --> 00:10:49.300
are successful in a certain
period of time, like 28 days.

00:10:49.300 --> 00:10:53.140
There's another concept, which
is a service-level agreement,

00:10:53.140 --> 00:10:56.160
which is basically a contract
that you have between you

00:10:56.160 --> 00:10:59.100
and the users of your service
that are paying for it in a way

00:10:59.100 --> 00:11:03.330
that, if you miss the SLO, if
you run out of error budget,

00:11:03.330 --> 00:11:08.330
then your users get a
certain type of compensation.

00:11:08.330 --> 00:11:11.710
So now that we have a
definition of an indicator,

00:11:11.710 --> 00:11:13.970
and we have a goal
for that indicator,

00:11:13.970 --> 00:11:17.600
like a certain level, how
can you find that level?

00:11:17.600 --> 00:11:22.130
So a way to find that first
goal, if you don't have

00:11:22.130 --> 00:11:25.910
any data of your service, like
your new introducing your SLOs,

00:11:25.910 --> 00:11:29.870
the way that you can do
is look at your past.

00:11:29.870 --> 00:11:33.530
The expected performance
that you have in the present

00:11:33.530 --> 00:11:35.750
is the performance
that your users have

00:11:35.750 --> 00:11:37.860
experienced in the recent past.

00:11:37.860 --> 00:11:39.680
So that's something
that you can use

00:11:39.680 --> 00:11:43.220
to define that first
level of performance

00:11:43.220 --> 00:11:45.160
that their service is providing.

00:11:45.160 --> 00:11:49.208
It's something that you should
not maintain ad infinitum.

00:11:49.208 --> 00:11:50.750
It's something that
you should change

00:11:50.750 --> 00:11:53.360
because expectations change.

00:11:53.360 --> 00:11:57.020
Past performance is not an
indicator of future results,

00:11:57.020 --> 00:12:01.450
so you should go back and see
how the system is performing

00:12:01.450 --> 00:12:03.260
at a certain time
in the present,

00:12:03.260 --> 00:12:08.180
only to change when the
environment changes.

00:12:08.180 --> 00:12:11.420
Imagine trying to get your
phone out of the pocket,

00:12:11.420 --> 00:12:16.820
and it's an iPhone 2, and then
connect it into a GPRS network

00:12:16.820 --> 00:12:19.355
right now and try to download
your favorite website.

00:12:19.355 --> 00:12:21.230
It will probably be a
nightmare, and then you

00:12:21.230 --> 00:12:22.760
will hate that experience.

00:12:22.760 --> 00:12:24.940
So things change, even
though you don't feel

00:12:24.940 --> 00:12:26.910
that the website is changing.

00:12:26.910 --> 00:12:28.430
They are loading
it with JavaScript,

00:12:28.430 --> 00:12:30.980
they're loading it with more
requests in the background.

00:12:30.980 --> 00:12:33.770
So things change in
time and, therefore, you

00:12:33.770 --> 00:12:36.230
should be analyzing how
those things are changing

00:12:36.230 --> 00:12:38.460
and get to the future.

00:12:38.460 --> 00:12:41.810
So now that we have a
certain level in which we

00:12:41.810 --> 00:12:44.770
can assume that the
service is going to fail,

00:12:44.770 --> 00:12:48.230
we have, for example, 99%--

00:12:48.230 --> 00:12:50.590
two nines-- for your service.

00:12:50.590 --> 00:12:54.970
You have a certain leeway in
which your service can fail.

00:12:54.970 --> 00:12:58.230
So the point is not
to avoid that failure.

00:12:58.230 --> 00:12:59.670
It's to embrace it.

00:12:59.670 --> 00:13:02.850
And once you embrace it, you
can use it for other things,

00:13:02.850 --> 00:13:05.670
like you can test
your environment.

00:13:05.670 --> 00:13:07.000
You can test your users.

00:13:07.000 --> 00:13:08.850
You can do AB experiments.

00:13:08.850 --> 00:13:15.160
You can increase the speed at
which you release new features.

00:13:15.160 --> 00:13:17.070
You can put that feature
that you were not

00:13:17.070 --> 00:13:19.570
expecting to put because
it's a little bit too risky,

00:13:19.570 --> 00:13:21.300
but then you can
put it there and see

00:13:21.300 --> 00:13:24.390
how your users are affected.

00:13:24.390 --> 00:13:28.260
So now we have what we
are trying to measure

00:13:28.260 --> 00:13:30.400
and how we are
trying to measure.

00:13:30.400 --> 00:13:33.900
So let's talk about how we
alert on those measurements

00:13:33.900 --> 00:13:36.030
that we have.

00:13:36.030 --> 00:13:38.520
First of all, we said
that a symptom is anything

00:13:38.520 --> 00:13:41.880
that you can
measure with an SLO.

00:13:41.880 --> 00:13:48.910
So a way to express
that SLO would

00:13:48.910 --> 00:13:51.980
be to measure the
uptime of your service.

00:13:51.980 --> 00:13:52.980
That's the availability.

00:13:52.980 --> 00:13:55.450
So you measure how
long it stays up

00:13:55.450 --> 00:13:59.980
compared to how long it
stays up and stays down.

00:13:59.980 --> 00:14:02.710
That is fantastic
for a single machine,

00:14:02.710 --> 00:14:05.350
but then we go back into the
complexity of the systems.

00:14:05.350 --> 00:14:07.520
Once you have your load
balancers to the front,

00:14:07.520 --> 00:14:10.330
you have all the 1,000 machines
responding in the back,

00:14:10.330 --> 00:14:13.810
it's almost impossible to
identify that binary in which

00:14:13.810 --> 00:14:16.190
you're identifying the uptime.

00:14:16.190 --> 00:14:20.950
So what do we do instead is
we define the availability

00:14:20.950 --> 00:14:23.650
in a complex system as
the amount of errors

00:14:23.650 --> 00:14:27.640
that you receive when
you are receiving traffic

00:14:27.640 --> 00:14:30.730
versus all the total requests
that are valid when you're

00:14:30.730 --> 00:14:34.000
receiving those requests.

00:14:34.000 --> 00:14:36.990
So there you have your
system, you have your SLOs.

00:14:36.990 --> 00:14:38.830
You put a prover.

00:14:38.830 --> 00:14:44.040
Your prover is testing your
machine every five minutes

00:14:44.040 --> 00:14:45.670
or every one minute.

00:14:45.670 --> 00:14:48.570
And then your system is telling
you that your system is set up,

00:14:48.570 --> 00:14:52.150
you have your availability
numbers that are at 100%.

00:14:52.150 --> 00:14:53.870
We're done, right?

00:14:53.870 --> 00:14:54.540
No.

00:14:54.540 --> 00:14:56.570
We're not right.

00:14:56.570 --> 00:14:59.870
Imagine that you have
a Chrome job that

00:14:59.870 --> 00:15:02.340
is running every
minute, and it's

00:15:02.340 --> 00:15:05.330
loading your service 10
seconds of every minute

00:15:05.330 --> 00:15:06.840
at beginning of the minute.

00:15:06.840 --> 00:15:08.360
And then you put
your prover that

00:15:08.360 --> 00:15:12.360
is only testing your
service every other minute,

00:15:12.360 --> 00:15:14.690
and they interleave
with each other in a way

00:15:14.690 --> 00:15:17.270
that only you are getting
the good pings, but not

00:15:17.270 --> 00:15:18.930
the bad pings.

00:15:18.930 --> 00:15:20.890
That is possible to happen.

00:15:20.890 --> 00:15:24.030
So in order to
increase the resolution

00:15:24.030 --> 00:15:26.810
that you obtained
from your prover,

00:15:26.810 --> 00:15:30.090
the thing that we recommend
is increasing the number

00:15:30.090 --> 00:15:32.160
that the resolution
of those props

00:15:32.160 --> 00:15:36.150
without increasing too much the
traffic that you're creating.

00:15:36.150 --> 00:15:40.710
Another way that we can use
is measure the availability

00:15:40.710 --> 00:15:42.200
at the application level.

00:15:42.200 --> 00:15:44.040
You instrument your
application to save

00:15:44.040 --> 00:15:46.800
logs on the request of your
receiving and the responses

00:15:46.800 --> 00:15:48.380
that you're providing.

00:15:48.380 --> 00:15:53.460
And you can increase the
coverage of the measurement

00:15:53.460 --> 00:15:54.570
of availability.

00:15:54.570 --> 00:15:56.670
The problem that you're
introducing over here

00:15:56.670 --> 00:15:59.970
is that you're only accounting
for the good requests

00:15:59.970 --> 00:16:01.050
that you receive.

00:16:01.050 --> 00:16:07.330
All the requests that died
at the load balancer level

00:16:07.330 --> 00:16:09.330
or died because the
network was down,

00:16:09.330 --> 00:16:12.620
you won't be able to see them.

00:16:12.620 --> 00:16:14.860
There's an expression
that I put over here.

00:16:14.860 --> 00:16:21.440
It's essentially a Prometheus
expression in which you just

00:16:21.440 --> 00:16:23.930
put this in your
Prometheus code,

00:16:23.930 --> 00:16:28.190
and you can see the availability
in the right numbers,

00:16:28.190 --> 00:16:31.370
essentially counting the
request that you receive,

00:16:31.370 --> 00:16:34.250
discarding the
requests that are 400,

00:16:34.250 --> 00:16:37.700
and then doing the calculation.

00:16:37.700 --> 00:16:42.410
Another possibility on how
to measure the availability

00:16:42.410 --> 00:16:45.560
is by doing it at the
load balancer level.

00:16:45.560 --> 00:16:48.200
You put your measurement
in the load balancer, which

00:16:48.200 --> 00:16:52.010
is account the ratio
that you receive, count

00:16:52.010 --> 00:16:55.070
the responses that you respond
versus all the responses

00:16:55.070 --> 00:16:57.530
that come back from your
back-end web servers,

00:16:57.530 --> 00:17:01.960
and are now 200, and voila,
you have a measurement.

00:17:01.960 --> 00:17:02.690
Right?

00:17:02.690 --> 00:17:03.710
We made it.

00:17:03.710 --> 00:17:06.560
We have all the symptoms
that are calculated.

00:17:06.560 --> 00:17:09.740
We are creating alerts that
are paging every time that we

00:17:09.740 --> 00:17:13.040
are seeing an error on the
availability of our system,

00:17:13.040 --> 00:17:15.940
and then happy users.

00:17:15.940 --> 00:17:17.609
Not so far.

00:17:17.609 --> 00:17:20.359
Once you start paging
for every single error

00:17:20.359 --> 00:17:22.250
that your service
is saying, you're

00:17:22.250 --> 00:17:25.940
basically counting every
drop as if it were the same.

00:17:25.940 --> 00:17:28.250
And that's not reality.

00:17:28.250 --> 00:17:31.270
A machine that is dying in the
background, your load balancer

00:17:31.270 --> 00:17:34.550
is not fast enough to
move to a healthy machine,

00:17:34.550 --> 00:17:36.560
your high-availability
service is not

00:17:36.560 --> 00:17:38.240
accounting for those
little glitches

00:17:38.240 --> 00:17:39.360
that happen over there.

00:17:39.360 --> 00:17:41.450
You don't want to page
for every single failure

00:17:41.450 --> 00:17:42.930
that occurs in your system.

00:17:42.930 --> 00:17:45.650
What you want to page
is for the SLO burn.

00:17:45.650 --> 00:17:48.990
It's the ratio at which the
error is being produced.

00:17:48.990 --> 00:17:51.830
So big floods of
errors are accounted

00:17:51.830 --> 00:17:54.470
for, small errors
occurring in isolation

00:17:54.470 --> 00:17:56.330
are not accounted for.

00:17:56.330 --> 00:17:58.050
How do we do that?

00:17:58.050 --> 00:18:01.640
We introduce a
little bit of math.

00:18:01.640 --> 00:18:03.140
The thing that you
want to calculate

00:18:03.140 --> 00:18:05.940
is the burn rate
at which you have--

00:18:05.940 --> 00:18:11.060
you define a threshold in which
your error rate is basically

00:18:11.060 --> 00:18:16.230
burning your entire error
budget at the end of the window.

00:18:16.230 --> 00:18:20.180
In this case, if we have
an average QPS of 1,000,

00:18:20.180 --> 00:18:22.850
what we can do is, if
we have an SLO of 99

00:18:22.850 --> 00:18:24.860
over a period of a
week, we calculate

00:18:24.860 --> 00:18:29.960
that we have a number of
604 million total queries,

00:18:29.960 --> 00:18:32.600
and therefore we have a
number of permissible errors

00:18:32.600 --> 00:18:35.750
of 6 million-plus.

00:18:35.750 --> 00:18:39.500
The idea of calculating
the fast burn error rate

00:18:39.500 --> 00:18:43.805
is by calculating that
threshold on how many QPSs

00:18:43.805 --> 00:18:45.140
are per second.

00:18:45.140 --> 00:18:46.880
In this case, the
threshold would

00:18:46.880 --> 00:18:51.440
be about 70 errors per second
that your system is producing.

00:18:51.440 --> 00:18:55.070
So what you do next is
defined over that threshold.

00:18:55.070 --> 00:18:56.760
You define two regions.

00:18:56.760 --> 00:19:01.340
The one region in which your
fast burning of error budget

00:19:01.340 --> 00:19:03.530
is going to consume
the entire error

00:19:03.530 --> 00:19:05.300
budget at the end
of your window,

00:19:05.300 --> 00:19:09.050
and then the area in which you
have a slow burn but at the end

00:19:09.050 --> 00:19:10.580
of the error window--

00:19:10.580 --> 00:19:12.380
at the end of the
time window, you're

00:19:12.380 --> 00:19:16.170
going to just burn a smaller
amount of that error budget.

00:19:16.170 --> 00:19:19.490
However, consuming that error
budget in a small amount

00:19:19.490 --> 00:19:23.070
will also introduce
some problems,

00:19:23.070 --> 00:19:26.330
which is that you will not have
that error budget to experiment

00:19:26.330 --> 00:19:27.450
and to do other things.

00:19:27.450 --> 00:19:29.600
So what you want to do
is, in the second part,

00:19:29.600 --> 00:19:33.890
you want to introduce a
level of notification,

00:19:33.890 --> 00:19:36.140
like send a ticket to
your operational team

00:19:36.140 --> 00:19:38.365
so that on Monday,
when they get to work,

00:19:38.365 --> 00:19:39.490
they can take a look at it.

00:19:39.490 --> 00:19:41.450
But they don't have to
respond immediately.

00:19:41.450 --> 00:19:45.140
On the other hand, when you
receive a fast burn alert,

00:19:45.140 --> 00:19:48.290
that means that you are
affecting your users in a way

00:19:48.290 --> 00:19:50.690
that your error budget
is going to be consumed

00:19:50.690 --> 00:19:52.010
by the end of that window.

00:19:52.010 --> 00:19:54.290
And therefore, it's
something that the system

00:19:54.290 --> 00:19:55.870
is being affected by.

00:19:55.870 --> 00:19:58.070
It's something that is--

00:19:58.070 --> 00:20:00.980
if it goes immediately
to failure, that line,

00:20:00.980 --> 00:20:02.380
that blue line--

00:20:02.380 --> 00:20:06.920
by the way, I didn't ask,
but anybody is color-blind?

00:20:06.920 --> 00:20:12.050
Anyway, the line on the top
that says fast burn, that is,

00:20:12.050 --> 00:20:14.190
in this case, a
line that is very

00:20:14.190 --> 00:20:17.190
similar to the threshold of
your error budget consumption.

00:20:17.190 --> 00:20:20.820
But if you have a
full outage, that line

00:20:20.820 --> 00:20:22.890
probably is almost vertical.

00:20:22.890 --> 00:20:25.560
Unless you're in the middle
of the night, and then

00:20:25.560 --> 00:20:28.133
you're just receiving
very small QPS.

00:20:28.133 --> 00:20:29.550
But still, it's
something that you

00:20:29.550 --> 00:20:31.650
would like to respond,
because the moment

00:20:31.650 --> 00:20:35.070
that you increase the QPS, that
line will be almost vertical

00:20:35.070 --> 00:20:39.368
and will be consuming your
error budget almost immediately.

00:20:39.368 --> 00:20:41.160
There's an expression
that I put over here.

00:20:41.160 --> 00:20:42.300
It's basically Prometheus.

00:20:42.300 --> 00:20:47.040
So it calculates
the errors that are

00:20:47.040 --> 00:20:51.660
produced in a period of hour.

00:20:51.660 --> 00:20:55.110
You would get paged when the
amount of increase errors

00:20:55.110 --> 00:20:58.620
in that period of an hour is
bigger than that threshold

00:20:58.620 --> 00:20:59.860
line of the error budget.

00:20:59.860 --> 00:21:02.520
And here, the
calculations that we

00:21:02.520 --> 00:21:04.380
put before, it's like
the number of QPS

00:21:04.380 --> 00:21:07.050
that we're receiving
in a period of time

00:21:07.050 --> 00:21:13.680
compared to your
target availability

00:21:13.680 --> 00:21:15.330
over a period of 24 hours.

00:21:18.110 --> 00:21:19.690
So putting it all together.

00:21:19.690 --> 00:21:25.060
Once you define what do you
want to measure in your service

00:21:25.060 --> 00:21:29.740
through your SLIs and your SLOs,
when you have your availability

00:21:29.740 --> 00:21:33.790
target that you have defined,
either through past experience

00:21:33.790 --> 00:21:38.680
or through observability of your
service, what you want to do

00:21:38.680 --> 00:21:44.590
is not forget observability,
like that old system

00:21:44.590 --> 00:21:48.970
that you're using right
now, which, by the way,

00:21:48.970 --> 00:21:58.470
I tend to call the
Nagios problem.

00:21:58.470 --> 00:22:01.260
When Nagios came out, it was
so easy to just install it

00:22:01.260 --> 00:22:03.900
in your machine,
put an SNMP server,

00:22:03.900 --> 00:22:05.630
and then immediately
you would get

00:22:05.630 --> 00:22:08.200
your CPU metrics,
your memory metrics,

00:22:08.200 --> 00:22:11.412
your [INAUDIBLE] consumption.

00:22:14.220 --> 00:22:15.480
Do not forget about that.

00:22:15.480 --> 00:22:20.430
It's something that might be
important to do correlation

00:22:20.430 --> 00:22:22.380
and to debugging your problems.

00:22:22.380 --> 00:22:29.430
But essentially, use the
four pillars of observability

00:22:29.430 --> 00:22:32.860
that we have described before.

00:22:32.860 --> 00:22:36.310
They are like [INAUDIBLE],,
metrics, traces, exceptions,

00:22:36.310 --> 00:22:37.800
core files.

00:22:37.800 --> 00:22:39.150
Collect that information.

00:22:39.150 --> 00:22:43.860
It will help you to
maintain that information

00:22:43.860 --> 00:22:48.500
to do correlation and causation
and root cause analysis.

00:22:48.500 --> 00:22:54.390
But move to the
system-based alert system.

00:22:54.390 --> 00:22:58.680
By doing that, you
transition slowly.

00:22:58.680 --> 00:23:01.110
Talk to the team that
is writing the software

00:23:01.110 --> 00:23:05.550
and see if you are
collecting logs.

00:23:05.550 --> 00:23:08.760
Talk to the people that are in
charge of the load balancers.

00:23:08.760 --> 00:23:12.420
If you are using Google
Cloud, many of the load

00:23:12.420 --> 00:23:14.960
balancers automatically
ingest that information

00:23:14.960 --> 00:23:16.260
into Stackdriver.

00:23:16.260 --> 00:23:18.810
So figure out what
is the information

00:23:18.810 --> 00:23:21.660
that you want to
page on, what are

00:23:21.660 --> 00:23:25.530
the metrics that are defining
how your service is operating.

00:23:25.530 --> 00:23:28.410
Introduce a couple
of SLIs on them,

00:23:28.410 --> 00:23:31.440
introduce a couple of SLOs
that define your objectives,

00:23:31.440 --> 00:23:34.650
and then start measuring
the availability.

00:23:34.650 --> 00:23:40.560
The transition assumes that your
teams are having all kinds of--

00:23:43.380 --> 00:23:45.450
it works in a coherent manner.

00:23:45.450 --> 00:23:48.900
Introducing SLOs and
targeting availability

00:23:48.900 --> 00:23:51.690
requires the development
team in order

00:23:51.690 --> 00:23:54.750
to do the thing that
we were talking about.

00:23:54.750 --> 00:23:57.510
Once you burn your
error budget and you

00:23:57.510 --> 00:24:03.240
don't have reliability
leeway left,

00:24:03.240 --> 00:24:05.700
you have to get your teams,
your development teams,

00:24:05.700 --> 00:24:08.560
to slow down in
putting new features,

00:24:08.560 --> 00:24:13.170
slow down in pushing
for those releases that

00:24:13.170 --> 00:24:16.440
may increase your risk.

00:24:16.440 --> 00:24:22.680
At Google, we have identified
that introducing new changes--

00:24:22.680 --> 00:24:24.570
even if it's
configuration changes,

00:24:24.570 --> 00:24:29.300
releasing new binaries--
increase the error that

00:24:29.300 --> 00:24:32.430
is being added to your system.

00:24:32.430 --> 00:24:35.000
So add a system-based alert.

00:24:35.000 --> 00:24:39.290
Lower this ability of
the system-based alert

00:24:39.290 --> 00:24:40.190
in the beginning.

00:24:40.190 --> 00:24:42.580
And then identify, if
you have an outage,

00:24:42.580 --> 00:24:46.010
that it's been paging through
your cost-based alert.

00:24:46.010 --> 00:24:48.340
If the system-based
alert is also paging,

00:24:48.340 --> 00:24:50.750
identify the moments
that your alerts

00:24:50.750 --> 00:24:53.795
are firing in the
cause-based alert

00:24:53.795 --> 00:24:57.320
but are not firing in
your system-based alert

00:24:57.320 --> 00:24:58.570
through your SLOs.

00:24:58.570 --> 00:25:00.320
Maybe your users are
happy and you're just

00:25:00.320 --> 00:25:04.190
paging your operators
without any reason.

00:25:04.190 --> 00:25:06.620
And slowly transition
to increase

00:25:06.620 --> 00:25:10.100
the severity of the
SLO-based alerting system

00:25:10.100 --> 00:25:15.590
and decrease the severity
of the cause-based alert

00:25:15.590 --> 00:25:18.590
once you feel more
comfortable with it.

00:25:18.590 --> 00:25:19.970
The next thing
that you should do

00:25:19.970 --> 00:25:24.830
is, those goals that we have
defined before, analyze them.

00:25:24.830 --> 00:25:28.460
The analysis is done through
critical user journeys.

00:25:28.460 --> 00:25:31.730
So get to your service,
talk to your developers,

00:25:31.730 --> 00:25:33.770
identify the things
that are extremely

00:25:33.770 --> 00:25:35.570
important to your service.

00:25:35.570 --> 00:25:39.650
And based on those,
identify some of the SLIs

00:25:39.650 --> 00:25:41.720
that increasingly will--

00:25:41.720 --> 00:25:44.360
sorry-- that, over time,
will increase in complexity.

00:25:44.360 --> 00:25:46.640
In the beginning, you
should aim for the basics,

00:25:46.640 --> 00:25:49.610
like the simple things that
provide you the biggest

00:25:49.610 --> 00:25:50.960
amount of information.

00:25:50.960 --> 00:25:55.700
But later on, you will
introduce more complex SLIs,

00:25:55.700 --> 00:25:59.750
like a system in which
you have to do a login.

00:25:59.750 --> 00:26:03.170
It may have certain interactions
with taking authorization

00:26:03.170 --> 00:26:06.450
tokens, bringing them back,
calling some other databases,

00:26:06.450 --> 00:26:09.460
some external
service for payment.

00:26:09.460 --> 00:26:12.950
So increase the
complexity of your service

00:26:12.950 --> 00:26:15.530
once you get
comfortable with that.

00:26:15.530 --> 00:26:18.290
The next thing that you do
is you rinse and repeat.

00:26:18.290 --> 00:26:21.910
SLOs should never
be set in stone.

00:26:21.910 --> 00:26:23.860
They change with your users.

00:26:23.860 --> 00:26:29.150
They change with the
development of your features

00:26:29.150 --> 00:26:31.970
and your service, with the
growth of your service,

00:26:31.970 --> 00:26:34.010
with the importance of it.

00:26:34.010 --> 00:26:36.545
You may be the leader in the
market and a lot of people

00:26:36.545 --> 00:26:38.010
are relying on you.

00:26:38.010 --> 00:26:43.790
So you need to move and create
that culture of revisiting

00:26:43.790 --> 00:26:46.280
and reviewing what you
have defined so that you

00:26:46.280 --> 00:26:49.106
can move as your service moves.

00:26:49.106 --> 00:26:52.610
And on that, there's
much more information

00:26:52.610 --> 00:26:57.680
about this topic in the two
books that we have published.

00:26:57.680 --> 00:27:00.980
There is a URL over there
where you can actually get

00:27:00.980 --> 00:27:04.040
the copies of the book in PDF.

00:27:04.040 --> 00:27:09.260
And we have also
a Coursera course

00:27:09.260 --> 00:27:15.710
that goes through the
entire workflow on how

00:27:15.710 --> 00:27:21.400
to develop those SLIs, those
SLOs, in much more detail.

00:27:21.400 --> 00:27:25.740
You have the URL so that you
can access it over there.

00:27:25.740 --> 00:27:30.090
And with that, you
made it to the end.

00:27:30.090 --> 00:27:32.490
[APPLAUSE]

00:27:32.490 --> 00:27:36.440
[MUSIC PLAYING]

